course: dictionary
lesson: 8046
title: Formal words and feelings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Regrettable is an adjective, a describing word. It means unfortunate, something you wish had not happened. In «No more of those regrettable incidents with the police», the speaker wishes those events had never happened.
explain: The word is built from regret, which means feeling sorry about something. So a regrettable thing is one that deserves regret. Look for the root word inside a longer word, and its meaning often gives you a clue.
explain: Leaders, news reports and school letters use this word because it sounds calm and serious. It is softer than saying "bad" or "terrible". In a story, a character who talks this way may sound pompous, as if trying to sound important.
fact: Regrettable means unfortunate, something you wish had not happened.
fact: Regrettable comes from regret, which means feeling sorry about something.
fact: A school letter might say «a regrettable incident» to describe a bad event in a calm, formal way.
fact: Regrettable is more formal than bad or sad, so it sounds serious and polite.
fact: In a story, regrettable can soften bad news or make a character sound pompous.
exercise: «No more of those regrettable incidents with the police» What does regrettable mean here? | Happy and fun to remember | Quiet and small | *Unfortunate, things that should not have happened | Easy to fix in a minute
exercise: A school letter says «a regrettable incident». What is the letter telling parents? | The school has a new teacher | *Something bad happened and the school is sorry about it | The school is having a party | Nobody was there at all
exercise: Which word is the root of regrettable? | letter | *regret | police | incident
test: Why might a character in a story say «regrettable» instead of «bad»? | *It sounds formal and can soften bad news | It is a longer word with no meaning | It means happy and cheerful | It means the same as delicious
test: Regrettable is an adjective. What does an adjective do? | *It describes a noun | It names an action | It asks a question | It joins two sentences together
exam: What is the best meaning of regrettable in «a regrettable incident»? | Bright and shiny | Quick and tidy | *Unfortunate, something you wish had not happened | Very large and exciting
